# 7. MANUAL UNIT & CONVERSION SYSTEM — CRITICAL REQUIREMENT

## Do not make packaging assumptions.

The pharmacy user must manually configure the units and conversion quantities for each medicine.

The system must not assume:

- Every tablet strip contains 10 tablets.
- Every tablet strip contains 12 tablets.
- Every box contains 10 strips.
- Every box contains 20 strips.
- Every bottle contains a fixed quantity.
- Every medicine follows the same packaging structure.

These values can vary by medicine, manufacturer, supplier, and product packaging.

## 7.1 Manual unit creation

Authorized users must be able to manually configure units for individual medicines.

Examples of units:

- Tablet
- Capsule
- Strip
- Box
- Bottle
- Vial
- Ampoule
- Tube
- Piece
- Pack
- Sachet
- Millilitre
- Gram

The system must not assume that every medicine uses these units. Users must be able to add their required unit names.

## 7.2 Medicine-specific conversion configuration

Each medicine must have its own conversion configuration.

Example A:

Medicine A:

- Base Unit: Tablet
- 1 Strip = 10 Tablets
- 1 Box = 100 Tablets

Example B:

Medicine B:

- Base Unit: Tablet
- 1 Strip = 12 Tablets
- 1 Box = 60 Tablets

Example C:

Medicine C:

- Base Unit: Capsule
- 1 Strip = 6 Capsules
- 1 Box = 48 Capsules

These are examples only.

The system must never hardcode these values.

The authorized user manually enters the actual values during medicine setup or unit configuration.

## 7.3 Conversion storage

All inventory must be stored internally using a consistent base unit for each medicine.

Every configured unit must have a manually defined conversion quantity to the base unit.

Example:

Medicine A:

| Unit | Conversion to Base Unit |
|---|---:|
| Tablet | 1 |
| Strip | 10 |
| Box | 100 |

Medicine B:

| Unit | Conversion to Base Unit |
|---|---:|
| Tablet | 1 |
| Strip | 12 |
| Box | 60 |

The system should store direct conversion values to the base unit instead of relying on a fragile conversion chain.

For example:

Do not depend on:

Box → Strip → Tablet

Instead, store:

- Tablet = 1 base unit
- Strip = 10 base units
- Box = 100 base units

The actual values must be entered manually by the user.

## 7.4 Unit configuration interface

The medicine setup interface must allow authorized users to:

- Create a unit.
- Rename a unit.
- Define its conversion ratio.
- Select the base unit.
- Enable it for purchases.
- Enable it for sales.
- Disable an unused unit.
- Configure multiple purchasing units.
- Configure multiple selling units.
- View conversion relationships clearly.

Example UI:

Medicine: Product A

| Unit | Conversion | Purchase | Sale |
|---|---:|---|---|
| Tablet | 1 | No | Yes |
| Strip | 10 | Yes | Yes |
| Box | 100 | Yes | Yes |

The user must be able to add, remove, edit, and configure units according to their permissions.

## 7.5 Conversion validation

Reject:

- Zero conversion ratio
- Negative conversion ratio
- Invalid numeric values
- Invalid unit relationships
- Duplicate unit names for the same medicine where prohibited
- Unsafe precision
- Conversion configurations that could produce invalid stock calculations

Use Decimal-based calculations.

Do not use floating-point numbers for financial calculations or critical inventory conversion calculations.

## 7.6 Historical conversion snapshots

Whenever a purchase or sale is recorded, store the conversion ratio used at that time.

For example:

At the time of a purchase:

1 Box = 100 Tablets

Later, the pharmacy modifies the configuration:

1 Box = 120 Tablets

The previous purchase must still retain its original conversion value.

Changing a conversion configuration must never silently modify:

- Historical purchases
- Historical sales
- Historical stock movements
- Previously calculated inventory quantities
- Historical profit
- Previously generated invoices

Conversion changes must be controlled and audited.

